  "query_preview": "..Today...Current mesoanalysis and satellite imagery \nreveals a subtle shortwave and a weak 40kt jet at 500mb over \neastern Arizona. This has combined with marginal instability (100 \nJ/kg of MUCAPE) to allow for very isolated and weakly rotating \nthunderstorms across the White Mountains as of 2 AM MST this \nmorning. This is expected to end within a few hours as the mid- \nlevel pattern shifts northeastward. Into today, a deep upper-level\nlow will continue to rapidly dive southward along the West Coast \nwhile ridging over the central CONUS amplifies. This will help \nlift the weak shortwave fully out of Arizona, and brief subsidence\naloft will lead to suppressed daytime shower activity. Still a \nfew weak showers or even thunderstorms will remain possible \nespecially towards the Kaibab and the White Mountains.\n\nLate tonight through Thursday...Beginning late tonight, the West \nCoast low will begin to push eastward and carry a deep surge of \nsubtropical and tropical moisture to northern Arizona. This moisture \nsurge is somewhat enhanced due to continued active tropical cyclone \nactivity across the Eastern Pacific. PWAT values (a measure of \natmospheric moisture) are forecast to rise up to 0.8-1.1 inches, \nwell above normal for mid-October. As a result, there will\nwidespread showers and thunderstorms bringing a primary hazard of\nFlooding/Flash Flooding through the period. The current forecast \ncalls for the heaviest rainfall accumulations along/south of the \nMogollon Rim, with a 1.0-3.0 inches expected. Another area of \nconcern will be the Navajo Nation, where widespread rainfall \naccumulations of 1.0-2.0 inches are forecast. Localized heavy \nrainfall will remain possible even outside of the regions. As a \nresult, a Flood Watch has been issued for all of Northern and \nCentral Arizona from 5 AM MST Wednesday-11 PM MST Thursday (6 AM \nMDT Wednesday-12 AM MDT Friday). Have a way to receive Flash Flood\nWarnings and be prepared to take action should warnings be \nissued.\n\nThere also remains a Severe Thunderstorm threat. A strong 500mb jet \nstreak moves overhead through the period, and 50+ kts of shear are \nlikely. There still is a degree of uncertainty in the available \ninstability, however mesoscale models remain adamant that we will \nsee CAPE values 500-1500 J/kg from high surface dewpoints and \nsteepening mid-level lapse rates. If the higher instability \nmanifests, there will be severe hail and wind threats. The shear \nprofile also suggests potential for rotating storms, and even an \nisolated tornado threat. The SPC maintains a Marginal risk (category\n1 out of 5) of severe weather across all of Northern and Central \nAZ on Wednesday and Thursday, and even a Slight risk (2 out of 5) \nfor severe storms across the Eastern Mogollon Rim and White \nMountains on Wednesday. Besides heavy rain and thunderstorms, \nexpect gusty daytime winds up to 30-40 mph on Thursday, though \nlikely remaining below Advisory criteria.\n\nFriday and onward...Activity is expected to trend downward by Friday \ninto after the main trough axis pushes past Northern AZ and drier \nair moves in. Some slight chances for showers look to linger each \nday, but there is low confidence during this period. Expect lighter \nwinds and temperatures remaining below normal otherwise.",
